-5819-ac16-9bb291ee177e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187929 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{ds}{dt} =-\\frac{K}{s}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187929 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187929 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0012b0af-84b6-57ce-8d0f-a4ba12d4441a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187930 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{ds}{dt} =-K" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187930 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187930 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "131a8375-1c95-5622-9d91-b151bca3a40b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187931 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{ds}{dt} =-Ks^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187931 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187931 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "172882bf-7e00-5b26-a37a-6608394ffe09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187994 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10072" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187994 #edb/ref 1071 "Since the year $2000$, a herd of antelopes multiplies at a rate proportional to their population. Also, the number of antelopes in the herd decreases by $20$ antelopes per year due to predators. What differential equation describes the rate of change in the population of antelopes at time $t,$ where $t$ is the time (in years) since the year $2000?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187994 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "67d01a20-4e4b-5b03-a2a3-f4b21cd81662" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187994 #edb/ref 1074 "The rate of change in the number of antelopes per year increases in proportion to $P$ and decreases by $20.$ The differential equation that describes this is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}P}{\\textrm{d}t}=kP - 20,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a (positive) constant of proportionality." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187995 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10075" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187995 #edb/ref 1071 "The volume of a sphere increases at a rate inversely proportional to its radius. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ in this process." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187995 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1b0483b6-a42d-51fb-9ccd-fe6cb2a7acc1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187995 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of a sphere **increases** at a rate **inversely proportional** to its **radius**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a sphere* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}$ *increases* at a rate *inversely proportional to its radius.* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} = \\dfrac{k}{r},\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a sphere is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{r} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{r} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{(\\frac{k}{r})}{4\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{4\\pi {r}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK = \\dfrac{k}{4\\pi} > 0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{K}{r^3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187996 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10076" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187996 #edb/ref 1071 "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative $y'(x)$ is **inversely** proportional to the square of the distance from $P$ to the origin. The derivative at the point $(-1,2)$ equals $2.$ What differential equation describes this curve?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187996 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b657adbb-c3c4-54c2-8c2c-44ccc0bf9c3a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187996 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** $y'(x)$ is **inversely proportional** to the **square of the distance from $P$ to the origin.** *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *square of the distance from $P$ to the origin* is given by $x^2+y^2.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *inversely proportional to* the square of the distance from $P$ to the origin, we have \n\n$$\ny'(x) = k\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\ny'(x) = \\dfrac{k}{x^2+y^2}.\n$$\n\nIn addition, we're told that $y'(x) = 2$ when at the point $(-1, 2).$ We can use this to determine the value of the coefficient $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\frac{k}{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{k}{5} \\\\ k & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the differential equation describing the curve is\n\n$$\ny'(x)= \\dfrac{10}{x^2+y^2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188203 #edb/ref 1069 "e-524" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188203 #edb/ref 1071 "The area of a circle decreases at a rate that's proportional to its radius. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ in this process." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188203 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "012abf23-a2df-5479-a38f-3c76c099a39f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188203 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** of a circle **decreases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to its **radius**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area $A$ of a circle* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *proportional* to its *radius.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = -kr\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d t}.$\n\nThe area of a circle is given by \n\n$$\nA=\\pi r^2.\n$$\n\n \n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-kr \\\\ 2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-kr \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{kr}{2\\pi r} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{k}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK = \\dfrac{k}{2\\pi} > 0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}t} = -K.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188257 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188257 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188257 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188257 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188257 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188257 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188257 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188257 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188257 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188257 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188257 #edb/ref 1039 "Expressing Two-Dimensional Related Rates Using Differential Equations" #edb/ref 175